# ============================================
# .gitignore — this file tells Git "don't track these files"
# Think of it like a note on a box that says "don't pack this."
# ============================================

# --- Operating system junk files ---
# Windows creates this file in folders you look at in File Explorer
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini

# macOS creates this file in folders you look at in Finder
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride

# Linux file manager leftovers
.directory
.Trash-*

# --- Editor / IDE folders ---
# VS Code settings some people don't want shared
.vscode/
# JetBrains IDEs (WebStorm, IntelliJ, etc.)
.idea/
# Sublime Text project files
*.sublime-workspace
*.sublime-project

# --- Node.js stuff ---
# This is dev-only test tooling (Vitest, for the pure logic in
# logic.js) — the real, deployed site still has zero dependencies
# and zero build step.
#
# ELI5 on the two npm files, since only ONE of them is hidden:
#   node_modules/      -> HIDDEN. This is the giant folder npm fills
#                         with thousands of downloaded files. It can
#                         always be rebuilt by running "npm install",
#                         so there is no reason to upload it.
#   package.json       -> TRACKED. The short shopping list saying
#                         which tools this project uses.
#   package-lock.json  -> TRACKED, on purpose. This is the receipt
#                         recording the EXACT version of every single
#                         thing that got installed. Keeping it means
#                         a fresh "npm install" on another computer
#                         builds an identical setup instead of a
#                         slightly newer, possibly broken one.
# Neither tracked file is hidden here — they are only mentioned so
# it is obvious the omission is deliberate rather than forgotten.
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# --- Logs and temp files ---
*.log
*.tmp
*.temp
*.bak
*.swp

# --- Environment / secret files ---
# Just in case a mail service, form backend, or API key ever gets added later
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# --- Lighthouse / performance reports ---
# ELI5: Lighthouse is a free robot that visits the website, times
# how long everything takes, and hands back a report card. Each
# report is a single ~1 MB file, and it goes out of date the
# moment anything changes — so keeping every old one would just
# make this repo heavy for no reason. Keep them on your own
# computer; the README's "🏎️ Performance (Lighthouse)" section is
# where the actual findings get written down for good.
*.report.json
*.report.html
lighthouse-report*.json
lighthouse-report*.html
itzino_com-*.json
itzino_com-*.html
.lighthouseci/

# --- Full-size image originals ---
# ELI5: the photos on the live site are shrunk down small so the
# page loads fast (see the README's Performance section). If you
# keep the big original versions around while making the small
# ones, drop them in a folder called "originals" and they will
# stay on your computer instead of doubling the size of the repo.
#
# Important: this ONLY hides that one folder. The real website
# photos sitting next to index.html are still tracked normally —
# without them the live site would be a page of broken images.
originals/
*-original.*
*-full.*

# --- Local helper tool output ---
# "graphify-out" is a folder a helper tool makes on YOUR computer to draw a
# map of this project's code. It's just a helper picture for you, not part
# of the actual website, so Git doesn't need to remember it or send it to
# GitHub.
graphify-out/